.\" ABSD manual page source (mdoc(7)); rendered by scripts/render-man.sh.
.Dd September 27, 2026
.Dt DATE 1
.Os ABSD
.Sh NAME
.Nm date
.Nd print the date and time
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl u
.Op Cm +%s
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
prints the time now, as the kernel reads it from the machine's real-time
clock, in the form
.Bd -literal -offset indent
Sun Sep 27 05:34:12 UTC 2026
.Ed
.Pp
The time is always UTC and always whole seconds: ABSD declares the
real-time clock to hold UTC, has no time zones, and the clock has no
finer resolution.
.Fl u
is accepted and changes nothing.
.Cm +%s
prints the seconds since 1970-01-01 00:00:00 UTC instead.
.Pp
Nothing on ABSD sets or corrects the clock.
Under QEMU it starts from the host's UTC time
.Pq Ql -rtc base=utc ,
the default.
A physical machine's clock may hold local time, or drift;
.Nm
cannot tell.
.Pp
The same clock stamps the lines of the logs under
.Pa /var/log
.Pq Xr afterboot 8 .
Files carry no times at all; see
.Xr filesystem 7 .
.Sh EXIT STATUS
0 on success, 1 when the kernel refuses a reading (the clock gave no
stable, valid value: no time is made up), 2 for any other argument.
Setting the clock and other formats are not supported.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr uptime 1 ,
.Xr filesystem 7
